## Step 2: Identify the Pareto Front

For maximizing both E and PF, a point is non-dominated if no other point has both higher E and higher PF.

Key candidates sorted by PF descending:
- 19p5-0add-25deg-130s-NoN2 run1: E=351, PF=0.43 (single fit, unvalidated)
- 19-0add-10deg-240s-NoN2 run2: E=309, PF=0.37 (single fit)
- 17p5-0add-20deg-90s-N2 run4: E=502, PF=0.35 (OK, multi-fit)
- 17p5-0add-20deg-90s-N2 run5: E=530, PF=0.34 (OK, multi-fit)
- 17p5-0add-25deg-375s-N2 run2: E=419, PF=0.33 (single fit)
- 19p5-0add-5deg-0s-N2: E=567, PF=0.33 (single fit)
- 17p5-0add-10deg-15s-N2 run2: E=475, PF=0.33 (single fit)
- 18p5-0add-5deg-105s-N2: E=593, PF=0.33 (top gap)
- 19-0add-10deg-240s-NoN2 run1: E=516, PF=0.33 (wrinkle)
- 17-0add-10deg-60s-N2: E=382, PF=0.36 (OK)
- 17p5-0add-15deg-60s-N2 run1: E=520, PF=0.36 (OK)
- 17-0add-5deg-run2: E=547, PF=0.31 (OK)
- 17p5-0add-15deg-60s-N2 run2: E=696, PF=0.26 (OK)

Let me carefully construct the Pareto front from well-measured, good-quality experiments:

Starting from highest PF:
- **PF=0.36, E=520**: 17p5-0add-15deg-60s-N2 run1 — not dominated by anything with PF≥0.36 and E≥520
- **PF=0.35, E=502**: 17p5-0add-20deg-90s-N2 run4 — dominated by run1 (520>502, 0.36>0.35)? Yes, run1 dominates run4.
- **PF=0.34, E=530**: run5 — E=530>520 but PF=0.34<0.36, so not dominated by run1. Is run5 on front? Need to check if anything has E≥530 and PF≥0.34. 19p5-0add-5deg: E=567, PF=0.33 — PF<0.34, doesn't dominate. 17-0add-5deg-run2: E=547, PF=0.31 — PF<0.34. So run5 (E=530, PF=0.34) is non-dominated.
- **PF=0.36, E=382**: 17-0add-10deg-60s-N2 — dominated by run1 (520>382, 0.36=0.36)? Equal PF but higher E, so yes dominated.
- **PF=0.31, E=547**: 17-0add-5deg-run2 — dominated by run5 (530<547 but PF 0.34>0.31)? No single point dominates: run5 has lower E. Is there E≥547 and PF≥0.31? 17-0add-15deg-90s-N2 run2: E=640, PF=0.29 — PF<0.31. 17p5-0add-15deg-60s-N2 run2: E=696, PF=0.26 — PF<0.31. So E=547, PF=0.31 is non-dominated.
- **PF=0.29, E=640**: 17-0add-15deg-90s-N2 run2 — dominated? Need E≥640 and PF≥0.29. 17p5-0add-15deg-60s-N2 run2: E=696, PF=0.26 — PF<0.29. 17-0add-11deg-60s-N2: E=924, PF=0.23 — PF<0.29. So E=640, PF=0.29 is non-dominated.
- **PF=0.27, E=596**: 17-0add-10deg-75s-N2 — dominated by E=640, PF=0.29 (both higher). Yes, dominated.
- **PF=0.26, E=696**: 17p5-0add-15deg-60s-N2 run2 — dominated? Need E≥696 and PF≥0.26. 17-0add-11deg-60s-N2: E=924, PF=0.23 — PF<0.26. So E=696, PF=0.26 is non-dominated.
- **PF=0.23, E=924**: 17-0add-11deg-60s-N2 — CV=0.23 (high), single run. Non-dominated at high E end.

**Confirmed Pareto front (good quality, multi-fit where available):**
1. E=520, PF=0.36 — 17p5-0add-15deg-60s-N2 **run1** (mixing_temp=65, bath_temp=15, pullcast_speed=10, N2=True, wait=60s, nips=1800s, polymer=17.5)
2. E=530, PF=0.34 — 17p5-0add-20deg-90s-N2 **run5** (mixing_temp=50, bath_temp=20, pullcast_speed=7, N2=True, wait=90s, nips=1250s, polymer=17.5)
3. E=547, PF=0.31 — 17-0add-5deg-run2 (mixing_temp=25, bath_temp=5, pullcast_speed=10, N2=True, wait=30s, nips=1200s, polymer=17.0)
4. E=640, PF=0.29 — 17-0add-15deg-90s-N2 run2 (mixing_temp=65, bath_temp=15, pullcast_speed=10, N2=True, wait=90s, nips=1800s, polymer=17.0)
5. E=696, PF=0.26 — 17p5-0add-15deg-60s-N2 **run2** (mixing_temp=65, bath_temp=15, pullcast_speed=10, N2=True, wait=60s, nips=1800s, polymer=17.5)

