## Step 2: Identify Pareto-Dominant Candidates

Objectives: maximize E (bar) AND maximize PF (dimensionless). A point dominates another if it is ≥ on both objectives and strictly > on at least one.

Key observations:
- **High E, moderate PF**: 17p5-0add-5deg-550s run1 (778, 0.27), 17-0add-11deg-60s (924, 0.23 — very high CV/SD, unreliable), 17p5-0add-18deg run1 (937, 0.27 — single specimen)
- **High PF, moderate E**: 18-0add-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s run1 (477, 0.39), 19p5-0add-25deg run1 (351, 0.43), 19-0add-10deg-240s run2 (309, 0.37)
- **Balanced**: 17p5-0add-15deg-60s run1 (520, 0.36), 17p5-0add-20deg run4 (502, 0.35), 17p5-0add-20deg run5 (530, 0.34)

**Approximate Pareto front** (non-dominated, excluding unreliable single-specimen or extreme-CV entries):

| Candidate | E (bar) | PF | Reliability |
|---|---|---|---|
| 18-0add-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s run1 | 477 | 0.39 | 2 runs but run2 gave 340/0.32 — inconsistent |
| 17p5-0add-15deg-60s run1 | 520 | 0.36 | 4 runs, variable |
| 17p5-0add-20deg run4/5 | ~516 | 0.34–0.35 | multiple runs |
| 17p5-0add-5deg-550s run1 | 778 | 0.27 | run2 gave 343/0.35 — inconsistent |

## Step 3: Assess Validation Status

**17p5-0add-15deg-60s-N2-1800s** (mixing_temp=65, bath_temp=15, pullcast_speed=10, N2=True, wait=60s, nips=1800s, polymer=17.5):
- Run1: E=520, PF=0.36 (CV=0.09, good quality)
- Run2: E=696, PF=0.26 (CV=0.08, high SD on E=187, top-edge gap noted in quality)
- Run3: E=407, PF=0.32 (CV=0.21, gap at top noted)
- Run4: E=621, PF=0.27 (single specimen, CV=null)

Across 4 runs: E ranges 407–696, PF ranges 0.26–0.36. **High variability across synthesis repeats.** The high-PF result (run1: 0.36) is not consistently reproduced — runs 2 and 4 show PF=0.26–0.27. This condition is not confirmed as a reliable high-PF producer.

**17p5-0add-20deg-90s-N2-1250s** (mixing_temp=50, bath_temp=20, pullcast_speed=7, N2=True, wait=90s, nips=1250s, polymer=17.5):
- Run1 (warm start): E=683, PF=0.28 (single specimen)
- Run2: null/null (all fits failed)
- Run3: E=327, PF=0.32 (CV=0.04)
- Run4: E=502, PF=0.35 (CV=0.15)
- Run5: E=530, PF=0.34 (CV=0.09)

Runs 4 and 5 show reasonable agreement: E~516±20, PF~0.345±0.005. This is a promising balanced candidate with moderate E and good PF. Run3 is an outlier (low E). The run1 warm-start single-specimen result (683/0.28) differs substantially from runs 4–5.

**18-0add-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s**:
- Run1: E=477, PF=0.39 (CV=0.09, good)
- Run2: E=340, PF=0.32 (CV=0.09)
These two runs are inconsistent — PF dropped from 0.39 to 0.32, E dropped from 477 to 340. Not confirmed.

**17p5-0add-18deg-75s-N2-1800s**:
- Run1: E=937, PF=0.27 (single specimen, CV=null)
- Run2: E=675, PF=0.30 (CV=0.06, good quality but top-edge gap noted)
- Run3: E=601, PF=0.20 (single specimen, very low PF)
Inconsistent PF (0.20–0.30), and run1 is unreliable. Not a confirmed Pareto improvement.

## Step 4: Identify the Most Promising Direction

Looking at the data landscape:

**High PF region**: Conditions with PF ≥ 0.33 and reasonable E:
- 17p5-0add-20deg runs 4&5: E~516, PF~0.34–0.35 ✓ (most reproducible high-PF + decent E)
- 17p5-0add-15deg run1: E=520, PF=0.36 (but not reproduced in runs 2–4)
- 19-0add-10deg-240s run2: E=309, PF=0.37 (single specimen, low E)
- 18p5-0add-5deg-105s run1: E=593, PF=0.33 (CV=0.15)

**The 17p5-0add-20deg-90s-N2-1250s condition** (runs 4 & 5) is the most reproducibly confirmed balanced candidate: E≈516 bar, PF≈0.34–0.35, with good membrane quality. However, run3 gave E=327 which is concerning.

**Exploration opportunity**: The data shows a pattern where bath_temp around 15–20°C with polymer_wt=17–17.5 and moderate wait times tends to give higher PF. The condition 17p5-0add-15deg-60s gave PF=0.36 in run1 but was inconsistent. 

Looking at what hasn't been well-explored: **bath_temp=15°C with polymer_wt=17.5, longer wait times** (the 120s run gave anomalous results with CV=1.02 — likely a bad batch). The 60s wait at bath_temp=15 showed promise in run1.

**Key insight**: The 17p5-0add-20deg-90s-N2-1250s condition has 5 runs with runs 4&5 agreeing well (E~502–530, PF~0.34–0.35). This is the most confirmed balanced Pareto candidate. However, run3 (E=327) is an outlier suggesting some synthesis variability. With runs 4 and 5 agreeing, this is reasonably validated.

**New exploration**: To push toward higher E while maintaining PF ≥ 0.33, I should explore nearby conditions. The 18p5-0add-5deg-105s run1 gave E=593, PF=0.33 with run2 giving E=545, PF=0.29. The PF dropped in run2. 

Looking at the broader pattern: **bath_temp=5°C** tends to give lower PF (0.27–0.33), while **bath_temp=15–20°C** gives higher PF (0.32–0.39). Higher bath temp slows phase separation, allowing more pore development.

**Unexplored promising region**: polymer_wt=17.5, bath_temp=15°C, moderate wait ~75s (between the 60s and 90s explored), pullcast_speed=10, N2=True, nips=1800s. The 60s wait gave variable results; 90s at bath_temp=10 gave E=442, PF=0.31. At bath_temp=15, 60s gave PF=0.36 in one run. 

Actually, looking more carefully: **17p5-0add-20deg-90s-N2-1250s** with runs 4&5 showing E~516, PF~0.34–0.35 is the best confirmed balanced point. A 6th run would further confirm this. But with 2 agreeing runs (4&5) and good quality, this is reasonably established.

**Better strategy**: Explore a new condition that could improve on the Pareto front. The combination of moderate-high E (>550) with PF>0.33 has not been reliably achieved. 

Consider: **polymer_wt=17.5, bath_temp=20°C, wait=75s, pullcast_speed=10, N2=True, nips=1800s, mixing_temp=65°C**. This is close to the 17p5-0add-20deg-90s condition but with slightly shorter wait and higher pullcast speed. The 17-0add-20deg-75s gave E=362, PF=0.28 (poor, single specimen). 

Actually, let me reconsider. The 17p5-0add-20deg-90s runs 4&5 are the best confirmed balanced candidates. The question is whether to validate further or explore.

**The 17p5-0add-20deg-90s condition has 5 runs** (runs 1, 2-failed, 3, 4, 5). Runs 4 and 5 agree well. Run 3 is an outlier. This is reasonably validated with 2 independent agreeing runs. I should now **explore** to find something better.

**Exploration target**: To get higher E while keeping PF high, I should look at conditions that combine features of high-E and high-PF conditions:
- High E tends to come from: lower polymer_wt (17–17.5), bath_temp ~10–15°C, moderate wait times
- High PF tends to come from: bath_temp 15–20°C, moderate wait times

A promising unexplored combination: **polymer_wt=17.5, bath_temp=18°C, wait=90s, pullcast_speed=10, N2=True, nips=1800s, mixing_temp=65°C**. 

The 17p5-0add-18deg-75s runs showed E=675 (run2) with PF=0.30, and run3 gave E=601, PF=0.20. The PF is lower at 18°C than at 20°C. 

What about **bath_temp=20°C with polymer_wt=17.5 but different wait time**? The 90s wait gave PF=0.34–0.35. What about 60s wait at bath_temp=20°C? Not yet tested for 17.5 wt%.

