## Step 2: Identify the Pareto Front (Good Quality Only)

Excluding clearly unreliable observations (CV > 0.5, severe defects with coverage gaps that likely invalidate measurements, or single-specimen-only with extreme values):

**Key candidates for Pareto front** (maximize both modulus AND pore fraction):

Plotting mentally along the trade-off:
- **High modulus, lower pore fraction**: 17-11deg-60s (924, 0.23), 17p5-18deg-run1 (937, 0.27), 17p5-15deg-run2 (696, 0.26), 17-15deg-90s-run2 (640, 0.29), 17p5-15deg-run4 (621, 0.27)
- **Moderate modulus, moderate pore fraction**: 17p5-15deg-run1 (520, 0.36), 17-10deg-60s (382, 0.36), 19-10deg-240s-run2 (309, 0.37), 18-15deg-600s-run1 (477, 0.39)
- **High pore fraction, lower modulus**: 19p5-25deg-run1 (351, 0.43)

**Non-dominated candidates** (no other point beats both simultaneously):
- (924, 0.23) — 17-11deg: high modulus but very low pore fraction
- (640, 0.29) — 17-15deg-90s-run2: good modulus, moderate pore fraction
- (520, 0.36) — 17p5-15deg-run1: moderate modulus, good pore fraction
- (477, 0.39) — 18-15deg-600s-run1: lower modulus, high pore fraction (but top-gap defect in run1; run2 gave 340, 0.32 — inconsistent)
- (351, 0.43) — 19p5-25deg-run1: low modulus, highest pore fraction (single measurement, high CV)

The **balanced Pareto region** of most interest is around modulus ~500–700 bar with pore fraction ~0.30–0.36.

